# On-device data and storage

## Where data lives

| Data | Store |
|------|-------|
| Tokens, license keys, PINs, activation ids | `expo-secure-store` only — never SQLite, AsyncStorage, logs, or backups |
| Relational app data | `expo-sqlite` |
| Small preferences and flags | AsyncStorage (or MMKV if the project already has it) |
| Files, exports, backups, images | `expo-file-system` |

## SQLite

- Values always as bound parameters. Table and column names are never interpolated from input; when they must be dynamic, check them against a whitelist (`PRAGMA table_info`).
- One logical operation = one transaction (`withTransactionAsync` or the project's serialized wrapper). Multi-row saves (enroll many, generate schedules, bulk updates) must not leave partial results when a row fails.
- Schema changes are versioned migrations (`PRAGMA user_version`) that run once at boot and are safe on databases created by every shipped version.
- Index the columns used in list filters and joins; page large lists in SQL (`LIMIT`/`OFFSET` or keyset) instead of filtering in JS.
- Keep the UI responsive during long writes: chunk and yield between transactions only when each chunk leaves the database consistent.

## Backup and restore

- Export a versioned format (`version`, app version, table list). Exclude secrets and license data.
- Restore is destructive: validate the whole file first (version, required tables, known columns), write a safety snapshot of the current database, then replace inside one transaction or into a staged database that is swapped in on success. On failure, roll back or restore the snapshot and tell the user nothing was changed.
- After restore, reopen the connection and run migrations so older backups match the current schema.
